t Wind Pung if I get F4 Pung (unlikely) or Prevailing Wind Pung if I get F1 Pung (unlikely).  \n\n- Could also aim for \"Half Flush\" (6 points) plus others. Half Flush requires honors + only one suit. Right now I have W, T, B mixed, so not going Half Flush unless I discard all but W + honors, but W tiles are few (W2, W5, W9, plus W1-3 melded already). Maybe possible? But W suit is missing many tiles, and W5 and W9 are isolated.  \n\n---\n\n**Safe discards**: Since my hand is still far from ready and other players may be close to winning (next player has 2 melds already), I should discard safest tiles.  \n\nLooking at discards:  \n- Previous player discarded W2, W3, T2 etc.  \n- Next player discarded J1, J3, W3, etc.  \n\nDanger tiles: B1, B9, T8, W7, etc.? Need to see history:  \nAcross player discarded J2 once.  \nW7 was discarded by across, W8 discarded by next player.  \nW9 is in my hand, only 3 left in wall, but W9 is terminal \u2014 not safe because someone could be waiting for W9 for All Terminals etc.  \n\nDragons: J1 and J3 have been discarded, so J1 and J3 might be safer.  \n\nFrom unshown tiles: J1: 3 left, J3: 3 left, J2: 3 left \u2014 actually J2 has been discarded once, but I have two J2, so throwing J2 might be risky if someone wants Dragon Pung.  \n\nCheck wind discards: F2 has been discarded twice (next player and across player), so F2 might be relatively safe.  \n\n---\n\n**Key strategic thought:** F2 is not seat wind (North F4) or prevailing wind (East F1), so it's less likely to be needed by others for Pung. F2 discard history shows it's not highly sought.  \n\nIn my hand, F2 is isolated \u2014 no pair, no sequence possible. Discarding F2 reduces honor clutter, lets me focus on improving suit connections or dragon pairs.  \n\nOther candidates: J3 singleton (safe because already discarded by next player), J1 singleton (safe because also discarded), W9 terminal (riskier because terminal in many patterns).  \nBut among them, F2 is safest because it\u2019s a wind not matching seat/prevalent.  \n\n---\n\n**Promising moves:**  \n1. Play F2 \u2014 safe discard, reduces honors, keeps J2 pair for possible Dragon Pung.  \n2. Play J3 \u2014 also safe, keeps flexibility with suits.  \n3. Play W9 \u2014 riskier, but maybe okay if others don\u2019t need terminals.  \n\nGiven safety early in hand development, F2 seems best.  \n\n---\n\nEvaluation: F2 is safest discard; it avoids giving others needed winds for Pung, and my hand needs more draws to shape into a scoring hand.  \nReason: F2 is neither seat wind nor prevailing wind, has been discarded before, and in my hand it's a lone honor with no immediate pairing/sequence potential. Keeping J2 pair for possible Dragon Pung later is better.  \n\n---\n\n**Answer: Play F2**  \n\n---\n\n**Final output:**  \nEvaluation: F2 is safest discard; others may need seat/prevalent winds or dragons more.  \nReason: F2 is not Seat Wind (North F4) or Prevailing Wind (East F1), has been discarded before, and discarding it keeps flexibility to develop Dragon Pungs or suit connections.  \nAnswer: Play F2"}
